    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so my monstera deliciosa has been looking kinda rough lately. a few of the lower leaves are turning this weird sickly yellow color. i'm in seattle so it's pretty cloudy and damp here, so i wasn't sure if i'm overwatering it or if it's just actually hungry for nutrients? any idea what might be causing this?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Monstera leaves turning yellow, especially on the bottom, are most commonly caused by overwatering or root rot rather than a nutrient deficiency, since your damp Seattle weather likely keeps soil moist longer than ideal. While too little light can also cause older leaves to pale, the combination of cloudiness and humidity often saturates the soil, preventing roots from breathing and causing them to rot. I recommend letting the soil dry out completely before watering again and inspecting the roots for mushiness or foul odors to rule out root issues. If the soil is already bone dry, it is more likely they are hungry, but try not to water for at least a day or two first to see if the symptoms improve.",
      "source": "target",
